<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Exeter</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Isca Dumnoniorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Exeter is a cathedral city on the River Exe and the county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a> in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It began around 55 CE as a fortress of the Roman legio II Augusta, and when the army moved on it became Isca Dumnoniorum, the civitas capital of the Dumnonii. It later became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> burh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seat and resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> in a siege of 1068.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/exeter.jpg" alt="Exeter" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simon Burchell · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exeter_Cathedral,_Devon_213.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
